> adventurer any time an approach within 2 spaces of the edge of the
> screen following a hallway, were made, or centered on the room when a
> new room is entered, but that is a separate set of changes from making
> use of the current larger resolution on graphics screen monitors. What
> it would do is let those of us of advancing years play the higher
> complexity games with more readible text sizes.)
>

Actually, this *is* available on most ports (not the Amiga one, for
some reason, and maybe not the Mac), via the CLIPPING define in config.h.
This doesn't give you a bigger level, it just lets you run the level on
a smaller screen (or the same sized screen with a bigger font). The
CLIPPING code is a bit messy and some messages come out funny, but it
generally works (I've used it to run NetHack on the Atari in low resolution,
and found it to be, on the whole, a pain -- it's *much* nicer to have the
entire level on screen at once).
